---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias14.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Business Segments vs Geographical Segments" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Primary vs Secondary Segment Reporting Format" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Identifying Reportable Segments" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Segment Measurement" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Segment Disclosure Items" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Why IAS 14 Was Replaced" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IAS 14 vs Modern IFRS 8" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="withdrawn"
    explanation="IAS 14 (issued 1981, revised 1997) required segment reporting based on an entity's identified business and geographical segments, using a risks and returns approach. It was superseded by IFRS 8 Operating Segments, effective for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2009, which replaced IAS 14's externally-defined segment concept with the management approach."
    historical="It is retained here for historical reference. For current segment reporting, always apply IFRS 8."
    link="/ifrs/ifrs8"
    linkText="IFRS 8 Operating Segments"
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why aggregate financial statements can mislead about diversified businesses</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework's relevance objective recognises that a diversified entity's aggregate financial statements can obscure important differences in risk, growth, and profitability across its various lines of business or geographical markets. IAS 14 addressed this by requiring segment information – but, unlike its eventual successor IFRS 8, it defined segments using externally specified "risks and returns" criteria, rather than the entity's own internal management structure.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 14 MAPPED TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Relevance</strong> – disaggregated segment information helps users understand risk/return profiles that aggregate figures conceal</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Comparability (attempted, imperfectly)</strong> – by defining segments externally (via risks and returns criteria) rather than via management's own view, IAS 14 aimed for greater cross-entity comparability than the later management approach – though this came at the cost of forcing entities to report segments possibly different from how they were actually internally managed</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Cost vs benefit tension</strong> – requiring entities to construct segment information not aligned with internal management reporting could impose significant additional preparation cost, a key criticism that led to IFRS 8's management approach reform</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 14's externally-defined segment approach was replaced specifically because it could require entities to construct and disclose segment information that did not correspond to how the business was actually run internally – a tension IFRS 8's management approach was designed to resolve.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Primary vs secondary reporting format – a structure IFRS 8 abandoned</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Format</span><span>Disclosure depth</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Primary format</span><span>Extensive disclosure (revenue, result, assets, liabilities, capex, depreciation)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Secondary format</span><span>Limited disclosure (revenue, assets, capex only)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;">An entity's dominant source of risks and returns (business or geography) determined which format was primary – a structural distinction IFRS 8 entirely abandoned in favour of a single unified reportable segment concept.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 14 covered</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 14 applied to entities whose equity or debt securities were publicly traded, and other economically significant entities choosing to disclose segment information – broadly similar in spirit to IFRS 8's own public accountability scope trigger.</div>
        <div class="note">This scope-trigger logic (public accountability) was carried forward largely unchanged into IFRS 8.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know (historical)</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Business segment (original)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A distinguishable component providing a single product/service, or group of related products/services, subject to risks/returns different from other business segments.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Geographical segment (original)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A distinguishable component providing products/services within a particular economic environment, subject to risks/returns different from other economic environments.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Business Segments vs Geographical Segments (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The "risks and returns" identification criteria</div>
        <div class="principle">Segments were identified based on factors such as: the nature of products/services; the nature of the production process; the type/class of customer; distribution methods; and, for geographical segments, similarity of economic/political conditions, currency risk, and exchange control regulations.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Identifying business segments under the risks/returns approach</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A diversified conglomerate operates a consumer electronics division and an agricultural equipment division – each facing materially different demand drivers, production processes, and customer bases, and thus different risk/return profiles.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">These would be identified as two separate business segments under IAS 14's risks/returns test – a conclusion likely similar to what the management approach (IFRS 8) would also produce in this case, since the divisions are genuinely distinct in both externally observable risk/return terms and (presumably) in internal management reporting.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">In many cases, IAS 14's externally-defined segments and IFRS 8's internally-defined segments would coincide – the practical divergence mattered most for entities whose internal management structure did not align neatly with externally observable risk/return groupings.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Primary vs Secondary Segment Reporting Format</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Determining which dimension dominates risk and returns</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An entity determined whether business segments or geographical segments were the dominant source of risks and returns, using that dimension as the primary reporting format (extensive disclosure), with the other dimension reported in less detail as the secondary format. This two-tier structure added genuine complexity that IFRS 8's single unified segment concept later eliminated entirely.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Identifying Reportable Segments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A 10% threshold – the direct ancestor of IFRS 8's own tests</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">A segment was reportable if its revenue, result, or assets were 10% or more of the combined total for all segments – essentially identical in structure to IFRS 8's own 10% quantitative thresholds (revenue, profit/loss, assets), carried forward with only minor refinement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Segment Measurement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Consistent accounting policies – the opposite of IFRS 8's approach</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Segment information under IAS 14 was measured using the same accounting policies adopted for the entity's consolidated financial statements – a direct contrast with IFRS 8's later approach, which permits segment measures to reflect whatever basis management uses internally, even if that differs from IFRS. This is one of the most significant substantive differences between the two standards.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Segment Disclosure Items</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Extensive primary format disclosures</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Segment revenue (external and inter-segment), segment result, segment assets, and segment liabilities</span>
          <span>Cost of property, plant, and equipment/intangible assets acquired during the period (capital expenditure)</span>
          <span>Depreciation and amortisation expense</span>
          <span>A reconciliation of segment totals to consolidated entity totals</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Why IAS 14 Was Replaced</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Convergence with US GAAP, and cost-effectiveness concerns</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IAS 14's externally-defined "risks and returns" segment model diverged from the US GAAP "management approach" (ASC 280, itself pre-dating IFRS 8). As part of the IASB's short-term convergence project with the FASB, IAS 14 was replaced by IFRS 8, deliberately modelled closely on the existing US GAAP standard – improving international comparability while also reducing preparer cost, since segment information could now be sourced directly from existing internal management reports rather than a separately constructed external classification.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures under IAS 14</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Factors used to identify business and geographical segments</span>
          <span>Types of products/services in each business segment</span>
          <span>Composition of each geographical segment</span>
          <span>Extensive primary format financial information (Section 7)</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement mattered most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Identifying business and geographical segments using the risks/returns criteria</span>
              <span>Determining which dimension (business or geography) was the dominant primary format</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Allocating shared costs/assets between segments, using consolidated accounting policies</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">The externally-defined segment identification exercise under IAS 14 was widely criticised as expensive and sometimes artificial – requiring entities to construct segment views that did not always correspond to how the business was genuinely managed internally.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IAS 14 vs Modern IFRS 8</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IAS 14 (1997 revision) vs current IFRS 8</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 14 (withdrawn)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">Current (IFRS 8)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Segment identification basis</span><span>Externally-defined "risks and returns" criteria</span><span>The management approach – how the CODM actually reviews the business internally</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Measurement basis</span><span>Must match consolidated accounting policies</span><span>Follows whatever basis management reports internally (may be non-IFRS)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Primary/secondary format structure</span><span>Two-tier structure based on dominant risk source</span><span>Abolished – single, unified reportable segment concept, plus mandatory entity-wide disclosures regardless of segment structure</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>10% quantitative thresholds</span><span>Revenue, result, assets</span><span>Same three tests, largely unchanged</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The single biggest change was philosophical – moving from an externally imposed classification to the internally-driven management approach, directly converging with, and modelled on, the pre-existing US GAAP standard.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping (Historical, Under IAS 14)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Segment revenue, result, assets, liabilities</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Using consolidated entity accounting policies</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Reconciliation to consolidated totals</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Since segment measures used the same policies, reconciling items were typically limited to unallocated items</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Primary vs secondary format disclosure</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Extensive vs limited detail, per dominant risk source</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 8</span><span>The direct successor, replacing the risks/returns approach with the management approach</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 1</span><span>Supplied the general presentation framework within which segment disclosures were made</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 5</span><span>Applicable segments needed to be identified for discontinued operations, even under the older IAS 14 regime</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Risks and returns approach", definition: "IAS 14's method of identifying segments based on externally observable differences in risk/return profiles, rather than internal management structure." },
      { term: "Primary/secondary reporting format", definition: "A two-tier disclosure structure based on whether business or geographical segmentation was the dominant source of risks and returns – abolished under IFRS 8." },
      { term: "Business segment", definition: "A distinguishable component providing a single product/service, or group of related products/services, subject to risks/returns different from other business segments." },
      { term: "Geographical segment", definition: "A distinguishable component providing products/services within a particular economic environment, subject to risks/returns different from other economic environments." },
      { term: "Reportable segment", definition: "A segment whose revenue, result, or assets equal 10% or more of the combined total for all segments, meeting IAS 14's quantitative threshold for separate disclosure." },
      { term: "Dominant source of risks and returns", definition: "The factor (business or geography) that most significantly influences the entity's risk/return profile, determining which segment dimension receives primary reporting format treatment." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
